# IdleRacer — AI Development Rules

## Core Principle

IdleRacer is a production Unity game intended primarily for iOS, with future support for Android and Web wherever practical.

The game is a mobile-first incremental / idle racing RPG.

The player does not directly drive the car during the main gameplay loop.

The car races automatically while the player manages:
- equipment,
- item generation,
- upgrades,
- skills,
- companions,
- trainers,
- refinement,
- dungeons,
- prestige,
- race disciplines,
- PvP Arena,
- collections,
- long-term progression.

All code must be maintainable, modular, testable, data-driven and suitable for a long-lived game.

Never optimise for completing a task quickly at the expense of architecture.

---

## Technology

- Unity 6.
- C# for game logic.
- URP.
- iOS is the primary deployment target.
- Future Android and Web support should remain possible.
- Avoid platform-specific logic in core gameplay systems.
- Unity Gaming Services is the preferred backend direction when online systems are introduced.
- Do not add third-party packages without explicit approval.

---

## Game Philosophy

The core loop must remain simple:

Auto Race
→ Earn Gold and Wheels
→ Generate Equipment
→ Upgrade Car
→ Progress Campaign
→ Unlock Systems
→ Reach Progression Wall
→ Improve Build
→ Progress Further
→ Prestige
→ Repeat Faster and Deeper

The player should always have:
- something happening now,
- something to upgrade soon,
- a medium-term unlock,
- a long-term objective.

The top portion of the screen should remain visually active with racing.

The bottom portion of the screen should provide progression interaction and decisions.

The game should avoid requiring steering, braking or manual driving input in the core loop.

Skills may support:
- full auto activation,
- optional manual activation.

Nitrous and normal race behaviour should be automated unless a future explicitly approved mode changes this.

---

## Architecture

Gameplay logic and visual presentation must remain separate.

The race simulation must never depend on:
- animations,
- particle systems,
- cameras,
- visual GameObjects,
- UI elements.

Race results must be calculable from input data.

Core systems should be usable without loading a Unity scene wherever practical.

Prefer plain C# classes for domain logic.

Use MonoBehaviour only when Unity lifecycle, scene behaviour or rendering integration is genuinely required.

Use ScriptableObjects for static content/configuration where appropriate.

Do not use MonoBehaviour as a general-purpose data container.

Do not create a monolithic GameManager.

Avoid global mutable state.

Avoid singleton managers unless explicitly approved.

Prefer dependency injection through constructors, interfaces or explicit composition where practical.

---

## System Boundaries

Keep these systems conceptually independent:

- Race Simulation
- Race Visualisation
- Campaign Progression
- Race Disciplines
- Equipment
- Item Generation
- Auto Build
- Equipment Refinement
- Equipment Slot Upgrades
- Currency / Economy
- Skills
- Companions
- Trainers
- Dungeons
- Dynamic Events
- Collections
- Prestige / Awakening
- Prestige Tree
- PvP Arena
- Save System
- Offline Progression
- Backend Services
- UI

Systems may communicate through:
- clearly defined interfaces,
- immutable data contracts,
- events,
- application services.

Do not create hidden dependencies.

---

## Race Simulation

The race simulation is the authoritative source of race outcomes.

Visuals represent the simulation result.

Visual animations must never determine the winner.

The simulator should eventually support:

- Main Campaign
- Drag Racing
- Circuit Racing
- Dungeon Races
- Dynamic Event Races
- PvP Arena Races

using the same underlying race simulation architecture.

PvP must support asynchronous opponent build snapshots.

Race simulations should support deterministic random seeds when randomness is required.

The simulation must eventually be able to account for:
- acceleration,
- top speed,
- grip,
- cornering,
- braking,
- gear shifts,
- nitrous,
- skills,
- track type,
- track length,
- corners,
- build modifiers,
- prestige modifiers,
- companion effects,
- trainer effects.

Do not add all of these at once.

Build the simulation incrementally.

---

## Campaign Structure

The campaign is fully autoplay.

The initial structure is:

Normal:
- 1-1 to 1-10
- 2-1 to 2-20
- 3-1 to 3-30

Hard:
- 1-1 to 1-10
- 2-1 to 2-20
- 3-1 to 3-30

Hell:
- 1-1 to 1-10
- 2-1 to 2-20
- 3-1 to 3-30

Additional difficulties may be added later.

When the player wins:
- progress automatically to the next stage.

When the player loses:
- remain on the same stage,
- automatically retry.

Campaign progression must unlock systems gradually.

Do not expose all systems at the start.

---

## Race Disciplines

Separate progression systems should eventually exist for:

### Drag Racing
Rewards:
- acceleration,
- launch,
- top speed,
- gear shifting,
- nitrous.

### Circuit Racing
Rewards:
- grip,
- braking,
- cornering,
- acceleration out of corners.

Future race disciplines may include:
- rally,
- endurance,
- weather-specific racing.

Do not implement future disciplines without approval.

---

## Currencies

Core currencies currently planned:

### Gold
Purpose:
- permanent equipment slot upgrades.

Gold should not normally be invested directly into temporary equipment items.

### Wheels
Purpose:
- one Wheel per item-generation attempt by default.

Wheels can be earned from:
- active racing,
- stage progression,
- race completion,
- bosses,
- events,
- offline progression.

### Refinement Currency
Purpose:
- refine specific equipment items with stars.

Primary source:
- refinement dungeon.

### Prestige Currency
Purpose:
- permanent Prestige Tree progression.

Primary source:
- Awakening / Prestige.

Do not introduce new currencies without explicit approval.

Every currency transaction must go through a central economy abstraction eventually.

UI must not directly modify currency.

---

## Equipment

There are eight equipment slots.

Current planned slots:

- Engine
- Turbo
- Gearbox
- Tyres
- Suspension
- ECU
- Exhaust
- Fuel System

Slot progression and individual item progression are separate systems.

Gold upgrades equipment slots permanently.

Individual generated items can have:

- slot type,
- rarity,
- base stats,
- percentage stats,
- affixes,
- tags,
- refinement stars,
- unique identity / item ID.

Do not silently destroy or replace player equipment.

Items should eventually support locking.

---

## Item Generator

The player creates equipment through an Item Creator / Part Creator system.

Default cost:
- 1 Wheel per generated item.

The Item Creator has a level.

Higher Item Creator levels improve rarity odds.

Rarity probabilities must be data-driven.

The UI must eventually show the exact real rarity probabilities.

Displayed probabilities must exactly match actual generation probabilities.

Never implement hidden drop-rate manipulation.

Potential rarity ladder may include:

- Common
- Uncommon
- Rare
- Epic
- Legendary
- Mythic

Final rarity names are subject to game design approval.

Do not hard-code final probabilities yet.

---

## Auto Build

Manual item generation exists from the start.

Auto Build unlocks from campaign progression.

Planned milestone behaviour:

- Complete Normal 1-10 → Auto Build unlocked, 1 item per cycle
- Complete Normal 2-20 → 2 items per cycle
- Complete Normal 3-30 → 3 items per cycle
- Complete Hard 1-10 → 4 items per cycle
- Complete Hard 2-20 → 5 items per cycle
- Complete Hard 3-30 → 6 items per cycle
- Complete Hell 1-10 → 7 items per cycle
- Complete Hell 2-20 → 8 items per cycle
- Complete Hell 3-30 → potentially 10 items per cycle

These values are currently design targets and must remain configuration-driven.

Auto Build:
- does not give free items,
- still consumes Wheels,
- must not change rarity odds,
- only changes throughput / convenience.

Future Auto Build filters may include:
- auto-dismantle by rarity,
- stopping on high-rarity drops,
- review tray.

Do not implement these without approval.

---

## Equipment Slot Upgrades

Gold upgrades the slot itself.

Example:
- Engine Slot Level remains even if the equipped Engine item changes.

This permanent progression prevents Gold investment from being wasted when equipment is replaced.

Slot upgrade effects should be data-driven.

Slot milestones should eventually provide meaningful bonuses, not only linear stat increases.

Examples:
- level 10 milestone,
- level 25 milestone,
- level 50 milestone,
- level 100 mastery milestone.

Final values and milestone effects must not be hard-coded without design approval.

---

## Refinement

Refinement applies to the individual item.

Current planned refinement:

0 stars:
- base stats

1 star:
- +5% all stats on that item

2 stars:
- +10%

3 stars:
- +15%

4 stars:
- +20%

5 stars:
- +25%

Refinement cost should increase per star.

Refinement currency primarily comes from a dedicated dungeon.

Refinement values and costs must be data-driven.

The game should eventually support a fair way to recover or transfer at least part of refinement investment when replacing valuable gear.

Final recovery percentage is not yet locked.

Do not invent it.

---

## Item Tags and Synergies

Equipment may eventually contain tags such as:

- Acceleration
- TopSpeed
- Turbo
- Street
- Circuit
- Drag
- Skill
- Grip

Tags may contribute to flexible synergy bonuses.

Avoid rigid traditional set-only design if possible.

The goal is theorycrafting and build variety.

Do not implement tag synergies until the base equipment system is stable.

---

## Builds

The game should support meaningful specialisation.

Example build directions:

- Acceleration
- Top Speed
- Nitro
- Grip
- Circuit
- Skill-focused
- Hybrid

A player should not be able to maximise every stat equally.

Trade-offs are desirable.

Example:
- high acceleration may come at the cost of top speed,
- high top speed may come at the cost of launch performance.

These trade-offs should be implemented through data-driven modifiers rather than scattered conditional code.

---

## Skills

Skills may activate automatically.

The player may optionally choose manual activation for supported skills.

Skills should not be required to make basic campaign autoplay function.

Skills may eventually:
- modify acceleration,
- modify speed,
- generate nitrous,
- activate temporary buffs,
- interact with race states.

Do not implement skills until the core race simulation and equipment loop are working.

---

## Companions

Companions may appear visually during races and provide passive or triggered effects.

Examples may include:
- drone,
- support vehicle,
- motorbike,
- pursuit car.

Companions should be data-driven.

Do not tightly couple companion logic to race visuals.

---

## Trainers

Trainers provide passive strategic bonuses.

Players may eventually equip a limited number.

Trainer effects should use the same central modifier architecture as other bonuses.

Do not create unique hard-coded stat paths for each trainer.

---

## Dungeons

Dungeons are limited-attempt activities.

Current design direction:
- approximately 2 free attempts per day for relevant dungeons.

Different dungeons may reward:
- Refinement Currency
- Skill resources
- Companion resources
- Trainer resources
- Gold
- Wheels

Each dungeon should use racing gameplay rather than being a static claim screen.

Daily attempt limits and rewards must be configuration-driven.

---

## Dynamic Race Events

Campaign autoplay should eventually include unexpected events to reduce repetition.

Possible events:

- Rival Challenger
- Parts Truck
- Golden Car
- Police Chase
- Sudden Rain
- Special Boss

These should:
- remain automated,
- visually change the race,
- provide bonus rewards,
- break repetition.

Do not implement dynamic events until the core campaign loop works.

---

## PvP Arena

PvP is asynchronous.

It is not real-time multiplayer.

Players race AI-controlled snapshots of other players' builds.

A PvP snapshot may eventually contain:

- car,
- equipment,
- refinement,
- slot levels,
- skills,
- companions,
- trainers,
- Prestige Tree effects,
- calculated stats,
- Arena rating.

PvP must be designed for server validation.

Never trust client-submitted:
- race result,
- rating changes,
- leaderboard score,
- currency rewards.

Arena should have:
- real-time or frequently updated ladder,
- rating,
- ranks,
- seasonal rewards.

Possible ranks:
- Bronze
- Silver
- Gold
- Platinum
- Diamond
- Master
- Grandmaster
- Legend

Exact final ranks are subject to approval.

Players may eventually maintain:
- Drag loadout,
- Circuit loadout,
- Balanced loadout,
- PvP Defence loadout.

Do not implement PvP until backend architecture is introduced.

---

## Prestige / Awakening

Prestige unlocks after a major progression milestone.

Current target:
- completion of Hard 3-30.

Once unlocked:
- Prestige remains available,
- the player chooses when to Prestige,
- Prestige is never forced.

Prestige should award Prestige Currency based on how far the player has progressed.

Prestige must have a preview showing:
- what resets,
- what is kept,
- exact Prestige Currency earned.

Prestige must require explicit confirmation.

Do not finalise reset rules without explicit game design approval.

Current likely direction:

Potentially reset:
- Campaign progress
- Gold
- Equipment Slot Levels
- Current Wheels

Potentially keep:
- Equipment
- Refinement
- Skills
- Companions
- Trainers
- Item Creator Level
- Prestige Tree
- Collections

These are not final.

Do not implement reset behaviour until explicitly approved.

---

## Prestige Tree

Prestige Currency is spent on a large interconnected passive tree.

The design philosophy is inspired by deep ARPG passive trees, but must remain original.

The tree should support:
- many paths,
- cross-path builds,
- small passive nodes,
- major nodes,
- build-changing keystones.

Example branches:
- Acceleration
- Top Speed
- Nitro
- Grip
- Equipment
- Skills
- Economy
- Circuit
- Drag

Keystones should create meaningful trade-offs.

Example:
+ large acceleration
- lower maximum speed

Do not hard-code keystone logic throughout gameplay systems.

Prestige bonuses must use central modifier structures.

---

## Temporary Prestige-Run Traits

Future design may include temporary Driver Traits selected during a Prestige run.

These reset on the next Prestige.

Example directions:
- Launch Specialist
- Nitro Maniac
- Corner King

These provide run-to-run variation.

Do not implement until explicitly approved.

---

## Post-Prestige Progression

Prestige should make earlier content dramatically faster.

Potential features:
- domination victories,
- automatic stage skipping,
- multi-stage skipping based on victory margin.

The player should visibly feel stronger on repeat runs.

Do not make the player manually grind identical early content at the same speed forever.

---

## Offline Progression

The player should earn resources while offline.

Likely rewards:
- Gold
- Wheels

Offline progress should be based on validated progression state.

Offline reward caps should be configuration-driven.

Do not trust device time for valuable online/competitive rewards when server time becomes available.

Offline progress must eventually be testable independently from UI.

---

## Collections

The game may include collection progress for:

- cars,
- equipment,
- companions,
- skills,
- bosses,
- tracks.

Collections may provide small permanent bonuses.

Collection bonuses must remain secondary to main progression.

---

## Save Data

Every save schema must have a version.

Never remove or rename saved-data fields without migration handling.

Save migrations must be implemented when schema changes.

Core saved data should eventually include:

- currencies,
- campaign progress,
- equipment inventory,
- equipped items,
- equipment slot levels,
- Item Creator level,
- refinement,
- unlocks,
- skills,
- companions,
- trainers,
- Prestige Tree,
- collections,
- offline timestamp.

Do not introduce cloud save yet unless explicitly instructed.

Start with clean local save architecture when save implementation begins.

---

## Data-Driven Design

Never hard-code progression values directly into gameplay classes when they belong to configuration.

Examples:

- stage difficulty,
- enemy stats,
- Gold rewards,
- Wheel rewards,
- Item Creator rarity odds,
- Auto Build unlocks,
- refinement costs,
- slot upgrade costs,
- Prestige rewards,
- Prestige Tree costs,
- dungeon rewards,
- track modifiers,
- offline caps.

Game balance must be adjustable without rewriting core logic.

---

## Modifier Architecture

All systems that modify car performance should eventually use a shared modifier pipeline.

Sources may include:

- equipment,
- slot upgrades,
- refinement,
- item affixes,
- item tags,
- skills,
- companions,
- trainers,
- Prestige Tree,
- temporary traits,
- dungeon modifiers,
- track modifiers.

Do not create separate ad hoc calculations for every feature.

Prefer:
Base Stats
→ Flat Modifiers
→ Additive Percentage Modifiers
→ Multiplicative Modifiers
→ Conditional Modifiers

The exact mathematical formula must be documented before implementation.

---

## Economy

Currency changes must eventually pass through a central Economy Service.

Never directly modify player currency from UI code.

Every currency transaction should record:

- currency type,
- amount,
- source or reason.

Examples:
- RaceReward
- StageFirstClear
- OfflineReward
- ItemGenerationCost
- SlotUpgradeCost
- DungeonReward
- PrestigeReward

This makes balancing and debugging possible.

---

## UI

UI must not contain business logic.

Buttons call services, commands or application-layer methods.

UI reads state and displays results.

The intended core screen layout is:

Top approximately 40–50%:
- live race gameplay.

Bottom approximately 50–60%:
- progression UI,
- Item Creator,
- equipment,
- upgrades,
- navigation.

The race should continue visually while the player interacts with lower-screen progression systems wherever practical.

UI must support:
- different iPhone screen sizes,
- safe areas,
- portrait orientation as the current intended direction unless explicitly changed.

Avoid fixed pixel positioning when responsive layout can be used.

---

## Code Quality

Use clear, descriptive naming.

Prefer composition over deep inheritance.

Avoid unnecessary abstractions.

Follow SOLID principles where they provide real practical value.

Methods should have one clear responsibility.

Avoid classes larger than approximately 500 lines.

Refactor before systems become monolithic.

Avoid duplicated logic.

Public APIs should have XML documentation when behaviour is not obvious.

Use nullable awareness where practical.

Handle errors explicitly.

Never silently swallow exceptions.

---

## Cursor Behaviour

Before implementing any feature:

1. Inspect the relevant existing architecture.
2. Read GAME_DESIGN.md.
3. Read ARCHITECTURE.md.
4. Read any relevant system documentation.
5. Identify files that need modification.
6. Explain the intended change before modifying code when the task is non-trivial.
7. Modify only files required for the task.
8. Do not refactor unrelated code.
9. Compile after implementation.
10. Fix all compilation errors introduced.
11. Run relevant tests.
12. Report exactly what changed.

Never rewrite an entire system merely to add one feature unless explicitly instructed.

Never delete existing functionality without explicit approval.

Never invent missing product requirements when they affect:
- economy,
- progression,
- monetisation,
- PvP,
- prestige,
- save data.

When requirements are ambiguous:
- preserve existing behaviour,
- flag the ambiguity.

---

## Testing

Core gameplay systems require automated tests.

At minimum, eventually test:

- race calculations,
- race determinism,
- item rarity generation,
- stat modifier stacking,
- equipment refinement,
- slot upgrades,
- currency transactions,
- Prestige calculations,
- offline progression,
- save migrations.

Random systems must support deterministic seeded testing.

Bug fixes should include regression tests whenever practical.

---

## Performance

This is a mobile-first game.

Mobile battery usage and thermal performance are first-class concerns.

Avoid:
- unnecessary per-frame allocations,
- excessive Update methods,
- repeated scene searches,
- unnecessary physics,
- expensive runtime reflection.

Do not use FindObjectOfType-style searches repeatedly in gameplay loops.

Use object pooling for frequently spawned visual objects.

Profile before performing complex optimisation.

The race simulation should not require full physics simulation unless explicitly approved.

---

## Git

Never commit directly to main automatically.

Never push without explicit user approval.

One feature per branch when feature development begins.

Commits should be small and logically focused.

Do not commit:
- Library
- Temp
- Logs
- Builds
- generated IDE files
- secrets.

Do not modify package versions unless required by an approved task.

---

## Documentation

Maintain these repository documents:

- GAME_DESIGN.md
- ARCHITECTURE.md
- ECONOMY.md
- ROADMAP.md
- SAVE_SCHEMA.md
- CHANGELOG.md

GAME_DESIGN.md is the source of truth for intended gameplay.

ARCHITECTURE.md is the source of truth for intended technical structure.

Code is the source of truth for current implementation.

If documentation and code conflict:
- report the conflict,
- do not silently guess.
